Z||000000||Fiero*101||11-19-2001||12:13 AM||||at least my mechanic told me that.<BR>how do i fix this problem.<BR>he told me somthing but i cant remember what it was. and it didnt sound right<BR>so any info is good info thanks<P>Fiero*101<P>------------------<BR>Fiero*101<BR>Gold 86 SE 2M6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycar101.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||11-19-2001||12:49 AM||||Fiero 101, get ready for "major nightmare 101". I dont care what anyone says,(and believe me, there will be somebody on here that says "I did mine and it wasnt that hard...I just <I>blah blah blah</I>...whatever, they got lucky), replacing the forward exhaust manifold (the one facing the rear window) is a major pain in the a$$. The rearward one (by the trunk) is no biggie.<BR>Why is it a pain? Crawl up on your engine and (try) to locate the bolts that hold it on...hope one doesnt break....yikes.<BR>I had to do it once...and once is all Ill ever do. Next time, it goes to Huntsville to the Fiero Factory.<BR>Regardless, you need to do it, or have it done. <B>NOT</B> a good thing to let it go like that...get it done, soon, and may God have mercy on your soul!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero*101||11-19-2001||12:54 AM||||Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>ok my next ?" is do i replace them or try and fix the ones i have?<P>If i do replace them where do i get them? and how much are we talking for them?<P>And what will happen if i dont fix this problem right away?<P>Fiero*101<P>------------------<BR>Fiero*101<BR>Gold 86 SE 2M6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycar101.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||G-Nasty||11-19-2001||12:57 AM||||mechanics charge about $50-70 per hr so You need to fix this yourself unless you have $300 laying around. Goto nearby autostore buy exhaust gaskets (they're really all the same) for 20-30 bucks depending on 2.5 (4cyl) or 2.8 (6cyl). Remove all necessary components to gain access to the bolts. It should take u only 3-5 hrs unless bolts BREAK off...if that happens, get holy water and sprinkle it all over. Just make sure to use penetrating oil on bolts the night before so this doesn't happen. Most bolts will come out fairly easily. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||11-19-2001||03:14 AM||||I did mine and it wasnt that hard...I just blah blah blah -- sorry My7, I just couldn't resist!!<P>No, it isn't easy but I have changed quite a few of them...... the way I do it is this -- I jack up the car and put it on jack stands in front of the front cradle bolts, then I remove the wheels, disconnect the upper engine strut (dogbone), place the jack under the rear of the cradle, remove the two rear cradle bolts and loswer the jack - the cradle swings down making the front manifold easier to get to - I don't even remove the decklid to do it this way. (Might as well replace the spark plugs while you are back there!)<P>Now, are the manifolds just leaking or are they cracked? Usually the front (closest to the rear window) cracks at a flange due to water rolling down the decklid when it is opened and hitting the hot manifold. If it is cracked it can be welded unless you want to buy another manifold......<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
